**8 mutations, 8 killed. No survivors.**

**Boundary r1 correction to M1's set.** The first draft described M1 as
"4 coal rows + the eleven/one set row" — a count of 5 over an enumeration of
4 distinct tests, because [eleven/one] is itself one of the rows whose title
mentions coal. The count was right and the SET was wrong, and the error
suppressed a genuinely useful fact now visible above: **M1 also fails
[iron-tier]**. A blanket rule returns `"natural"` before reaching any vacuity
test, so the v2-killer pair row kills the blanket rule as well. Caught by the
discipline in Method §3, which exists for exactly this. The loose "all four
coal rows" phrasing previously used for M8 is replaced by named rows
throughout — under it [eleven/one] counted as a coal row in one place and not
in another.

## The load-bearing pair — the point of the exercise

The design killed two single-keyed rules by counterexample. These two rows
exist so neither can return silently, and the mutation run confirms each
bites its OWN dead rule and nothing else:

- **M2 (live-set-only, dead v1) → exactly ONE failure**, and it is
  `coal stays constrained when the user EXCLUDES Constructor`. Ticking
  Constructor empties coal's live producer set; without the CONSTANT
  conjunct coal natural-izes and the user silently loses both the
  Charcoal/Biocoal picker and the "edit MACHINE EXCLUSIONS" hint — the
  regression at the hero item that the whole refinement exists to prevent.
- **M3 (constant-only, dead v2) → exactly ONE failure**, and it is
  `ore_iron stays constrained with the TIER lever when the user UN-EXCLUDES
  Converter below tier 9`. `iron_limestone` is a converter recipe unlocked at
  tier 9, so at tier ≤ 8 with the Converter re-enabled "raise TIER" is a REAL
  recovery; without the LIVE conjunct it is suppressed.

Neither row passes under both dead rules, and neither is doing the other's
job — the two failure sets are disjoint singletons. That is precisely the
bidirectionality the design asked for.

## Findings that required investigation

### M4 came out STRONGER than the spec predicted — checked, not banked

The spec says the union "is weaker (implied by either conjunct) and would
re-admit the coal regression". Observed: M4 fails **both** pair rows, not
just the coal one. Investigated rather than recorded as-is.

It is correct, and it follows from the spec's own algebra. `P(S) ⟺ Prod ⊆ S`,
so `P(CONST) ∨ P(live) ⇒ P(CONST ∪ live)` — the union fires wherever EITHER
conjunct holds. Coal @ Constructor-excluded has `P(live)` true, so the union
fires and coal natural-izes (the predicted regression). `ore_iron` @ Converter
un-excluded @ TIER ≤ 8 has `P(CONST)` true, so the union fires there too and
suppresses the tier lever. The union is weaker than the conjunction in both
directions at once; the spec named one instance, not the whole set. No
correction to the design is implied — the conjunction is what shipped.

### M6 initially SURVIVED — a real gap in the test set, now closed

**First run: M6 (drop the `isRawResource` guard) failed 0 of 907 tests.**
A surviving mutant on a load-bearing conjunct, so it was investigated instead
of being written up as a clean sweep.

Cause: spec item 4 nominates `polymer_resin` as the non-raw spot-pin, and
`polymer_resin` **cannot detect the guard's removal**. Its sole producer is
the `oil_refinery`, which is in neither exclusion set, so BOTH vacuity
conjuncts already fail for it — the rule is silent with or without the raw
flag. The spec's chosen example is a true statement about the 20 non-raw
items but a non-discriminating test.

Measured: dropping the guard natural-izes **fifteen of the 20** non-raw
constrained items. Named in full, because the names are the claim (boundary
r1 — the first draft said "14 of the 20" and then listed fifteen, and the
matching source comment silently dropped `turbo_fuel` from its parenthetical
to make 14 work; both reviewers independently re-derived the true split as
15/5, and this log's own closing section is titled "Named sets, not counts"):

> `packaged_water`, `packaged_oil`, `packaged_alumina`, `packaged_biofuel`,
> `packaged_ionized_fuel`, `packaged_nitric_acid`, `packaged_nitrogen_gas`,
> `packaged_oil_residue`, `packaged_rocket_fuel`, `packaged_sulfuric_acid`
> (the ten packager-only packaged fluids), `turbo_fuel`
> (`Recipe_PackagedTurboFuel_C`, also on the packager — this is the one the
> wrong count dropped), and the converter-only `dark_energy`,
> `ficsite_ingot`, `quantum_energy`, `time_crystal`.

The remaining **five** are safe either way, each having a producer outside
both exclusion sets: `compacted_coal` (assembler); `polymer_resin`,
`heavy_oil_residue`, `liquid_turbo_fuel` (oil refinery); and `fuel`, whose
alternate sits on the oil refinery.

Closed by ADDING a discriminating pin — `packaged_water` must stay
`constrained` with the `machine` lever — alongside the spec-mandated
`polymer_resin` row, which is retained. This strengthens spec item 4 rather
than departing from it: the spec's stated goal is "the 20 non-raw affected
items are unchanged", and the added row is the one that actually enforces it.
`packaged_water` is the right item on the merits too — unpackaging is a
genuine recovery (enable the Packager, get the water back out of a canister),
nothing like the degenerate ore→ore conversion this design suppresses.

**Re-run after the addition: M6 fails exactly 1 test — the new pin.** A
precise bite of one, not a cascade.

## Corroborating mutants

- **M5 (predicate deleted)** reproduces pre-change behaviour and fails
  [eleven/one] [iron/water] [s20-pin] [ui-empty] [ui-rate] [ui-natural]. This
  is the direct evidence that spec item 2's accepted UI change is genuinely
  tested — the "Nothing to build" assertions are not passing for some
  incidental reason — and, since boundary r1, that the IMPROVEMENT is pinned
  too: under M5, Iron Ore reverts to a constrained line on an ordinary Iron
  Plate chain and [ui-natural] goes red. (M5's count rose 5 → 6 when that row
  gained its assertions, which is the evidence they are discriminating rather
  than decorative.)
  Note what M5 does NOT fail: the metrics-block absence. That is why an
  assertion on it was dropped from [ui-rate] at boundary r1 as decorative —
  `view.isEmpty` is `proposal.stages.length === 0`, already true for an
  all-raw proposal before this change and untouched by it, so the `<dl>` was
  absent either way. [ui-rate] bites through the rate TEXT, which the
  CONSTRAINED LINE (`ChainBuilder.tsx:541`) used to emit.
- **M7 (gated instead of ungated)** fails [coal-tier] [iron-tier], pinning the
  spec's requirement that the predicate read the UNGATED world: gating alone
  must never natural-ize anything.
- **M8 (alternate-blind)** fails [eleven/one] [coal-alts] [coal-machine]
  [coal-tier], pinning the spec's "must use the ALTERNATE-INCLUSIVE
  `producerRecipesFor`" — `coal`'s alternates are exactly what an
  `effectiveDefaultRecipe` test cannot see.
- **M1 (blanket rule)** fails those same four PLUS [iron-tier], confirming both
  that the ticket's naive framing is caught and that the v2-killer row does
  double duty against the blanket rule.

## Boundary r2 — a pass-either-way ASSERTION inside a biting row

Mutation testing scores whole TESTS, so a row that already fails a mutant can
still harbour an assertion that never bites. [ui-natural]'s rate check was one:
as first written it matched `container.textContent`, and under M5 the
constrained line emits the byte-identical `"Iron Ore 90/min"`, so it could only
discriminate in concert with its sibling `.chain-builder-constrained` check.

Now scoped to the metrics block, it pins "on the plain RAW line" DIRECTLY
rather than by elimination. Verified by isolation probe rather than by
argument — siblings disabled, M5 applied — where it fails on its own with:

> `expected 'Σ POWER24 MWΣ MACHINES6RAW—' to contain 'Iron Ore 90/min'`

i.e. the RAW `<dd>` falling back to `"—"` (`ChainBuilder.tsx:466`) exactly as
claimed. No mutant count moved, because the row's verdict was never in doubt;
what changed is that each of its assertions now earns its place. Worth
recording as a distinct failure MODE: "all mutants killed" is a statement about
tests, not about the assertions inside them.

## Named sets, not counts

The central row asserts the two sets by name:

- natural: `liquid_oil`, `nitrogen_gas`, `ore_bauxite`, `ore_copper`,
  `ore_gold`, `ore_iron`, `ore_uranium`, `raw_quartz`, `sam`, `stone`,
  `sulfur`, `water`
- constrained: `coal`

(`sam` is the 13th raw-flagged item and reaches "natural" through the
pre-existing no-producer branch, not the new rule — 11 items move, matching
the design's measured 11-of-12.) No row in this change asserts a bare count
against the real catalog.

## Independent re-measurement of the design's ground truth

Re-derived from the bundled catalog before any code was written; every figure
in the spec's "Ground truth" section reproduced exactly: 13 raw-flagged items;
32 constrained under default exclusions, 12 of them raw-flagged; producers
converter- and/or packager-only for all 12 EXCEPT `coal`, which also carries
`alternate_coal_1` / `alternate_coal_2` on `constructor_mk1` at unlock tier 3;
`iron_limestone` on the converter at unlock tier 9.
